<Project>
  <!--
    ToolUp.Sdk meta-manifest. Imported by consuming apps' Directory.Packages.props:

        <Project>
          <PropertyGroup>
            <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
            <ToolUpSdkVersion>0.1.0</ToolUpSdkVersion>
          </PropertyGroup>
          <Import Project="$(NuGetPackageRoot)toolup.sdk\$(ToolUpSdkVersion)\build\ToolUp.Sdk.props" />
          <ItemGroup>
            <PackageVersion Include="..." Version="..." />  (non-ToolUp deps)
          </ItemGroup>
        </Project>

    Each consumer fsproj then adds <PackageReference Include="ToolUp.X" />
    (no Version attribute — versions live centrally) and resolves through
    this manifest at restore time.

    Every package shipped from this monorepo is listed below, each on
    its version group's line. Bumping <ToolUpSdkVersion> in the consumer's
    Directory.Packages.props upgrades the core in lockstep and moves every
    companion family to the version it shipped at beside that core release.
    To hold or advance one family on its own, set that family's property
    (for example <ToolUpAuditSinksVersion>) in the same PropertyGroup as
    <ToolUpSdkVersion>, ABOVE the Import: the manifest only supplies a
    family's version when the property is still empty. The families and
    their properties are in the generated region below. Per-package
    overrides are still possible — declaring a more specific
    <PackageVersion Include="ToolUp.X" Version="..." /> later in the
    consumer's Directory.Packages.props takes precedence.

    Note the illustrative entry above says "(non-ToolUp deps)" in plain
    parentheses rather than in a nested XML comment. It was a nested
    comment until Phase 326, and that made this ENTIRE FILE unloadable:
    XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so the outer comment
    terminated at the inner one and MSBuild refused the import with
    MSB4024, whose text is "An XML comment cannot contain" the offending
    pair — which cannot be quoted here, for the very reason the rule
    exists. Every consumer that imported this manifest failed at that
    error, before any version could resolve at all: the file resolved
    nothing, rather than resolving too little. Do not reintroduce a
    nested comment,
    or a literal double hyphen, anywhere in this file; the generator now
    refuses to write a manifest that does not parse, and the Build test
    pack asserts the committed one does.

    Phase 326 (2026-09-12): the entry list below is GENERATED from the
    projects the `Publish` target packs and pushes — see the marker
    comment above it for the regeneration command. It was hand-listed
    until this phase, and had fallen to 59 entries against 163 required
    (166 published, three excluded by shape): a consumer referencing any
    of the 105 absent ones under central package management got NU1008
    from the one file whose entire purpose is to prevent exactly that,
    and one entry named a package that never existed. The prose that
    used to sit between the entries
    is preserved here instead, because a comment interleaved with
    generated lines cannot survive the generator, and deleting it to make
    the file mechanical would have thrown away the rationale below.

      * ToolUp.Platform.UI (Phase 307) — the `Toolup.UIToolkit` component
        set, the icon primitives and the typed prop-helper family,
        promoted out of ToolUp.Platform.Client into a standalone package
        (same move, same reasons as Phase 344's Feliz.AgGrid split).
        `ToolUp.Platform.Client` still depends on it, so an existing
        consumer needs no fsproj change — it arrives transitively, and
        every namespace is preserved, so unlike 344 no compat re-export
        was needed (GP 11). Listed so a consumer that wants the toolkit
        ALONE can add one PackageReference with no version.

      * ToolUp.Feliz.AgGrid / ToolUp.Feliz.AgCharts (Phase 344, ids
        settled by Phase 754) — the Community bindings, promoted out of
        ToolUp.Platform.Client into standalone Apache-2.0 packages so a
        consumer that renders no grid pays for none of it (GP 13), and
        one that wants only the binding can take it without the SDK.
        `ToolUp.Platform.Client` still depends on both, so an existing
        consumer needs no fsproj change: they arrive transitively. The
        compat re-export that kept `open ToolUp.Platform.AgGrid` /
        `.AgChart` compiling was retired in Phase 815 — open
        `Feliz.AgGrid` / `Feliz.AgCharts` directly.

      * ToolUp.Feliz.AgGrid.Enterprise (Phase 344, id settled by Phase
        754) — the RENAMED `ToolUp.AgGridEnterprise`: same opt-in
        companion, same licensing posture (GP 2). A consumer on the old
        id changes exactly one PackageReference. The old id is
        deliberately absent — nothing produces a package by that name any
        more, and a PackageVersion for an id no feed can serve resolves
        to NU1101 at restore rather than to anything useful. The rename
        is recorded in ToolUp/SDK-ADOPTION.md. A stale entry of that kind
        is now REPORTED (as UNPUBLISHED) rather than left for a consumer
        to find.

      * Why the `ToolUp.` prefix on all three (Phase 754, operator
        2026-09-01). They packed as bare `Feliz.*` until then, and
        `Feliz.AgGrid` on nuget.org is Compositional-IT's unmaintained
        binding — the one this extraction supersedes — so the id was
        never claimable. Two consequences met here: the repo's nuget.org
        Trusted Publishing policy is registered against the `ToolUp.*`
        glob, which the bare ids fall outside, and the template gate had
        already resolved one of them to a stranger's package (Phase 307,
        NU1603). The CLR namespaces did not move — a consumer still
        writes `open Feliz.AgGrid` — so only the PackageReference id
        changes. This generated list is derived from what the tree packs,
        so it follows the `<PackageId>` automatically; the note is here
        because the derivation cannot explain itself.
  -->
  <!-- BEGIN GENERATED - do not hand-edit below this line.
       Every id below is derived from the projects the `Publish` target packs
       and pushes (`src/**/*.fsproj`, minus the reference-app directories,
       minus `<IsPackable>false</IsPackable>`).

       Regenerate by running the GenerateSdkManifest target of the repo's
       Build.fsproj. The command cannot be written out here: an XML comment
       cannot contain a double hyphen, and the invocation has two. It is in
       docs/migrations/326-sdk-manifest-reconcile.md, and in the message of
       every failure that asks for it.

       `SdkManifestTests` in the Build pack fails when this region and the
       tree disagree, so an added package cannot ship unadvertised and a
       removed one cannot linger here as an NU1101.

       Published, and deliberately NOT listed:
         - ToolUp.Cli: <PackAsTool> — installed from a tool manifest (`dotnet tool install`), never a <PackageReference>, so central package management never consults a version for it
         - ToolUp.RAG.StaticCorpus.Build: <PackAsTool> — installed from a tool manifest (`dotnet tool install`), never a <PackageReference>, so central package management never consults a version for it
         - ToolUp.Sdk: the meta-package that SHIPS this manifest — a consumer must already pin it in their own Directory.Packages.props for the import to resolve, so an entry here could never be the one that resolves it

       Each id resolves through ONE property, its version group's (declared in
       version-groups.json). Core packages read ToolUpSdkVersion, which the
       consumer sets. Each companion family reads its own property, defaulted
       to the version that family carried when this manifest was released, so a
       consumer pinning only ToolUpSdkVersion resolves every package; setting a
       family's property in Directory.Packages.props moves that family alone.
  -->
